						<node id="Inputs" />
						<!--Start Dialog Tutorial Edits-->

						<!--The "EffectComponents" section contains information about all the effect components for each line of dialog. Effect components control all the audiovisual elements that will play for a cinematic or line of dialog, and can include everything from voice lines, to camera angle changes, to VFX.-->
						<node id="EffectComponents">
							<children>
								<!--This is an effect component!-->
								<node id="EffectComponent">
									<!--Each effect component has a 'Type,' i.e. the kind of effect it produces.
									
									I'll be covering TLAttitudeEvent a bit more later! Right now, we're just going to cover some basics about effect components.-->
									<attribute id="Type" type="LSString" value="TLAttitudeEvent" />
									<!--This is unique to each effect component on the timeline! Generate a new UUID for each effect component you add.-->
									<attribute id="ID" type="guid" value="aa1b8c1d-75f5-4031-96ce-f4aaa1d64788" />
									<!--Most effect components have both a StartTime and EndTime, which list the start and end times for the component within the total timeline. However, the effect components for the first line of dialog/cinematic on the timeline usually won't have a StartTime, unless they start partway through the dialog!
									
									Most things in the timeline files start their numbering at 0, and the game usually doesn't list these starting 0 values.
                                    
                                    This includes PhaseIndexes! The first cinematic/line of dialog on a timeline is given PhaseIndex 0, which won't be listed. That's why this effect component has no PhaseIndex attribute! (Side note: Starting timelines at 0 is standard for video editing and animation, too!)-->
									<attribute id="EndTime" type="float" value="18.29" />
									<!--IsSnappedToEnd doesn't seem to do much when editing it in the XML. This might be something their engine uses to snap effect components to the ends of Phases, like when a video editor has clip snapping enabled.-->
									<attribute id="IsSnappedToEnd" type="bool" value="True" />
									<!--The 'Actor' in an effect component determines what character/object/camera/etc the effect will apply to! Most effect components can only apply to certain kinds of actors, though. TLAttitudeEvent will only apply to characters.-->
									<children>
										<node id="Actor">
											<!--The UUID of the actor the effect component will apply to. In this case, it'd be Astarion. This is his UUID listed in the TimelineSpeakers section of the file, which can be cross referenced in the 'speakerlist' section of the DialogsBinary file!-->
											<attribute id="UUID" type="guid" value="baa8d56b-3671-0f79-04fd-4345329cf311" />
										</node>
									</children>
								</node>




								<!--Some tips on navigation:

                                Searching for "EffectComponents" will bring you to the top of the timeline, and searching "TimelineSpeakers" will bring you to the end of it.

                                You can usually find all the effect components for a phase by searching for this, but with the PhaseIndex for the line listed instead of the 1 (unless you're looking for PhaseIndex 1, in which case, feel free to stop here!):
                                
                                    <attribute id="PhaseIndex" type="int64" value="1" />

                                
                                This is different for PhaseIndex 0, though. To find the effect components for PhaseIndex 0, you'll have to search for things around them. Try searching for PhaseIndex, and go to the first time it appears in the file. All the effect components above that won't have listed phase indexes, and will be part of PhaseIndex 0. (You could also search for that line of code I listed for PhaseIndex 1, but it's quicker to just search for PhaseIndex!)-->







								<!--Some tips on timeline ordering, and adding to the timeline:
                                
                                The start of the timeline isn't always the start of a scene, or the first line of dialog in it. The PhaseIndexes don't have to be in order! The game can skip around wherever in the dialog it needs to. And, in fact, I highly encourage you to take advantage of that, and add any new dialog/cinematics to the end of the timeline.
                                
                                This is both because it's easier, and to prevent having to adjust the timing of everything that comes after. Doing so can make it extremely hard to double check any potential errors!-->





								<!--End Dialog Tutorial Edits-->



								<node id="EffectComponent">
