                                <!--Start Dialog Tutorial Edits-->

                                <!--I won't be editing TLSwitchStageEvent or TLAnimation, but I will be explaining a bit about what they do!-->

								<node id="EffectComponent">
                                    <!--TLSwitchStageEvent allows you to switch between different "stages" in the Dialog Scene file, which will allow the game to set different positions for characters, cameras, lights, objects etc, that are different from their default positions. I've also provided an annotated Scene file for his romance dialog, which explains this a little bit more.
                                    
                                    TLSwitchStageEvent is usually used for animations that need the characters to be in specific positions, but it can be used to set different positions for things in a scene, generally!-->
									<attribute id="Type" type="LSString" value="TLSwitchStageEvent" />
									<attribute id="ID" type="guid" value="24fdcec2-6cc6-44a4-9a13-799383c29745" />
									<attribute id="StartTime" type="float" value="748.00006" />
									<attribute id="EndTime" type="float" value="766.11005" />
									<attribute id="PhaseIndex" type="int64" value="57" />
									<attribute id="IsSnappedToEnd" type="bool" value="True" />
									<children>
										<node id="Keys">
											<children>
												<node id="Key">
                                                    <!--The time the stage will be switched to.-->
													<attribute id="Time" type="float" value="748.00006" />
                                                    <!--This is almost always 3.-->
													<attribute id="InterpolationType" type="uint8" value="3" />
                                                    <!--The UUID of the stage being switched to, which can be found in the Scene file for the dialog.-->
													<attribute id="SwitchStageEventID" type="guid" value="e8ade219-bb13-45fb-bdb0-f22c38f19d3c" />
												</node>
												<node id="Key">
                                                    <!--The time to switch from the stage to the default positioning.-->
													<attribute id="Time" type="float" value="764.29004" />
													<attribute id="InterpolationType" type="uint8" value="3" />
                                                    <!--To stop using the positioning set by the scene, just remove the SwitchStageEventID attribute.-->
												</node>
											</children>
										</node>
									</children>
								</node>
								<node id="EffectComponent">
                                    <!--TLAnimation controls the animations an actor uses!-->
									<attribute id="Type" type="LSString" value="TLAnimation" />
									<attribute id="ID" type="guid" value="8089fba6-2dc8-4fe7-a7f3-83ccd9297878" />
									<attribute id="StartTime" type="float" value="748.00006" />
									<attribute id="EndTime" type="float" value="750.74005" />
									<attribute id="PhaseIndex" type="int64" value="57" />
                                    <!--The animation being used. This can be a "key" that links to animations for multiple different bodytypes and races in an AnimationSetBank, to allow characters to use an animation made specifically for their bodytype, or it can be the ID of an animation outside of an AnimationSetBank.-->
									<attribute id="AnimationSourceId" type="guid" value="25921323-0d66-4d97-a98e-065b1c9c57d8" />
                                    <!--This allows you to play the animation from a point partway through the start of it! It seems to allow you to "skip" over the specified amount of time, and play the animation from that point on.-->
									<attribute id="AnimationPlayStartOffset" type="double" value="0.8000000000000003" />
                                    <!--This is similar to the FadeIn attribute for TLVoice! Can enable tweening at the start of an animation, to smooth out transitions between the character's previous pose/animation and this current pose/animation.-->
									<attribute id="FadeIn" type="double" value="0" />
                                    <!--The same as above, but for the end of the animation.-->
									<attribute id="FadeOut" type="double" value="0" />
                                    <!--Seems to identify each of the animations used by a specific character! I'm not entirely sure if this is necessary to edit yet, or if it's another thing that's used in Larian's engine, and isn't really something modders need to worry about.-->
									<attribute id="AnimationGroup" type="guid" value="dda47e37-0022-4737-82fe-ea6a21967ba9" />
									<children>
                                        <!--The actor the animation will play for. I don't think this has to be a character! TLAnimation can also be used by props/set pieces/etc.-->
										<node id="Actor">
											<attribute id="UUID" type="guid" value="6c70c2df-ab95-d5c1-0db5-c863be615f73" />
										</node>

										<!--
                                        

                                        <node id="TargetTransform">
											<attribute id="Scale" type="float" value="1" />
											<attribute id="Position" type="fvec3" value="2.346196 2.27 -14.23988" />
											<attribute id="RotationQuat" type="fvec4" value="0 0.7928832 0 0.6093737" />
										</node>
                                        
                                        This wasn't necessary or present in the original animation, so I've commented it out. I just wanted to mention it, because it *can* sometimes be necessary for an animation to function (just not this one!) TargetTransform allows you to set new positioning for an actor for an animation! It allows you to change the actor's scale, change their position from their default one along the X, Y, and Z axes, and change their rotation in the form of RotationQuat/Quaternation.
                                        
                                        I've explained a bit more about positioning in the annotated files for TLTransform and the annotated Scene file I've provided, but I thought it'd be important to note as a possibility here, too.
                                        
                                        -->

									</children>
								</node>

                                <!--I do also want to take the time to talk about a couple attributes for animations that aren't featured here!


                                
                                <attribute id="AnimationPlayRate" type="double" value="0.8" />

                                AnimationPlayRate controls the speed that the animation plays! The value here is a multiplyer, which will determine how much faster or slower the animation will play. 0.8 would make the animation play a little slower than normal.




                                <attribute id="HoldAnimation" type="bool" value="True" />
                                
                                HoldAnimation seems to be able to let the animation loop and continue beyond the end of the TLAnimation effect component! There's also a similar "<attribute id="Continuous" type="bool" value="True" />" attribute, but I need to do more experimenting with both of them to say more definitively what they do, and the differences between them. HoldAnimation has been more helpful for looping animations so far, though!


                                -->


                                <!--End Dialog Tutorial Edits-->

								<node id="EffectComponent">
									<attribute id="Type" type="LSString" value="TLAnimation" />
									<attribute id="ID" type="guid" value="1ae504aa-ec60-4d4e-83c7-673cd22e28d3" />
									<attribute id="StartTime" type="float" value="748.00006" />
