								</node>

                                <!--Start Dialog Tutorial Edits-->

                                <!--In this section, I'll be editing the player's TLAttitudeEvent poses during Astarion's last line when you ask him what you are to him before completing his romance.-->

								<node id="EffectComponent">
                                    <!--TLAttitudeEvent controls different animations and poses used by the player and the Peanut characters during dialog, although you might be able to apply it to other characters, too.
                                    
                                    Think Tav crossing their arms, or shifting their pose during dialog, etc. for this.-->
									<attribute id="Type" type="LSString" value="TLAttitudeEvent" />
									<attribute id="ID" type="guid" value="0d5e2128-7375-456d-abec-b40223cb7268" />
									<attribute id="StartTime" type="float" value="1207.7059" />
									<attribute id="EndTime" type="float" value="1231.116" />
									<attribute id="PhaseIndex" type="int64" value="94" />
									<attribute id="IsSnappedToEnd" type="bool" value="True" />
									<children>
                                        <!--The character that will perform the pose/animation. In this case, the player.-->
										<node id="Actor">
											<attribute id="UUID" type="guid" value="6c70c2df-ab95-d5c1-0db5-c863be615f73" />
										</node>
										<node id="Keys">
											<children>
												<node id="Key">
                                                    <!--The time the pose will change.-->
													<attribute id="Time" type="float" value="1207.7059" />
													<attribute id="InterpolationType" type="uint8" value="3" />
                                                    <!--The pose/animation the character will switch to. These can be cross-referenced by searching for "Attitude" in character AnimationBanks, or looking to other dialog files as examples.
                                                    
                                                    A note: this UUID is a "key" that will refer to the animation in multiple animation banks, for multiple different races/bodytypes/etc. You'll likely want to reference that key, and not the animation ID itself, so the game can reference the proper animation for the character's bodytype! You can do this by searching for the ID for the animation, and finding where it's linked in an AnimationSetBank resource. The MapKey listed under the animation ID is what you're looking for, and what you'll want to link here!-->
													<attribute id="Pose" type="FixedString" value="7f6ccd67-aa7d-4803-ad7f-629c7783e83f" />
                                                    <!--The animation that will be used to transition from the character's previous pose to this one. I'd recommend looking at examples of TLAttitudeEvent in other files to find the proper transition.-->
													<attribute id="Transition" type="FixedString" value="375d49d9-707a-42fb-a7f5-7bccba35a6ea" />
												</node>

												<node id="Key">
                                                    <!--I'm adding another time for a pose shift for the demo here. In this instance, I want to change the player's pose during Astarion's 'But then, whatever in the world could you be?' line to a "confused" attitude pose. However, that line is part of a set of grouped dialog, so I had to cross reference this time from that line. (I've explained more on grouped dialog in the next section of annotations.)
                                                    
                                                    I set the time here to start partway through the line.-->
													<attribute id="Time" type="float" value="1229.3859" />
													<attribute id="InterpolationType" type="uint8" value="3" />
                                                    <!--The animation "key" for the confused pose.-->
													<attribute id="Pose" type="FixedString" value="7afbcfa6-55cb-4272-ba5b-d905175e4317" />
                                                    <!--The animation transition for the pose. This is actually the same as the one above! It seems like some transitions can be used for multiple different kinds of attitude poses. I cross referenced this from existing dialog files, which used the transition for both.-->
													<attribute id="Transition" type="FixedString" value="375d49d9-707a-42fb-a7f5-7bccba35a6ea" />
												</node>                                                
											</children>
										</node>
									</children>
								</node>
								<node id="EffectComponent">
									<attribute id="Type" type="LSString" value="TLAttitudeEvent" />
									<attribute id="ID" type="guid" value="a5bbed6c-1e4e-76af-1a37-b52b09982418" />
									<attribute id="StartTime" type="float" value="1207.7059" />
									<attribute id="EndTime" type="float" value="1231.116" />
									<attribute id="PhaseIndex" type="int64" value="94" />
									<attribute id="IsSnappedToEnd" type="bool" value="True" />
                                    <!--The IsMimicry tag is used for Peanut characters! This doesn't seem to do much when editing dialog on the XML level, though. You probably won't have to worry about this much! I'm guessing it's something Larian's game engine uses to duplicate things like poses and expressions onto peanut characters.-->
									<attribute id="IsMimicry" type="bool" value="True" />
									<children>
										<node id="Actor">
											<attribute id="UUID" type="guid" value="0b2d1337-faae-406d-881d-ae38f4777a5f" />
                                            <!--

                                            <attribute id="PeanutOverride" type="bool" value="True" />
                                           
                                           ^^^ Sometimes, this line is placed under a peanut actor for things like TLAttitudeEvent and TLEmotionEvent!

                                           This seems to be used for peanut characters when they have unique expressions and poses that don't match the player character. And it seems to be mutually exclusive with IsMimicry, but it also doesn't seem to affect the game when editing it on the XML level. You can set expressions and poses that don't match the player's, even with the IsMimicry tag enabled in the XML!

                                            -->
										</node>
                                        <!--I left this peanut character's poses unchanged.-->
										<node id="Keys">
											<children>
												<node id="Key">
													<attribute id="Time" type="float" value="1207.9679" />
													<attribute id="InterpolationType" type="uint8" value="3" />
													<attribute id="Pose" type="FixedString" value="7f6ccd67-aa7d-4803-ad7f-629c7783e83f" />
													<attribute id="Transition" type="FixedString" value="375d49d9-707a-42fb-a7f5-7bccba35a6ea" />
												</node>
											</children>
										</node>
									</children>
								</node>

                                <!--End Dialog Tutorial Edits-->

