                                <!--Start Dialog tutorial Edits-->

                                <!--This is the line I wanted my edits to TLAttitudeEvent to apply to! But, because it's in a set of grouped dialog, I have to make my edits to the TLAttitude entry for the entire PhaseIndex, which encompasses all dialog nodes in the group. Grouped dialog links multiple dialog nodes under the same PhaseIndex, and I'm sure it's way easier to deal with dialog like that in Larian's game engine. But it's a bit of a pain in the ass for modders, quite frankly. If you're making more extensive edits to grouped dialog, you can try separating out the lines to make things a little easier in the long run. I won't be doing that here, though.
                                
                                To separate the lines, you'll need to give them their own PhaseIndex and corresponding entries in the Phases and TimelinePhases sections of the file, and replicate any other effect components that applied to the line, especially their TLEmotionEvent, TLAttitudeEvent, and TLLookAtEvent entries. This will save you some headaches in the long run, especially when making adjustments to timing, and is necessary to disable parts of grouped dialog. It's already a bit of a headache in and of itself, though.
                                
                                For this, I just cross referenced the Start and EndTimes of the line with the timing of my TLAttitudeEvent changes.-->

								<node id="EffectComponent">
									<attribute id="Type" type="LSString" value="TLVoice" />
									<attribute id="ID" type="guid" value="8170a539-0eab-4283-b9f2-856b85c0ebc9" />
                                    <!--The time I needed to cross reference in the TLAttitudeEvent effect component to make my changes.-->
									<attribute id="StartTime" type="float" value="1223.6859" />
									<attribute id="EndTime" type="float" value="1231.116" />
									<attribute id="PhaseIndex" type="int64" value="94" />
									<attribute id="IsSnappedToEnd" type="bool" value="True" />
                                    <!--Refers to Astarion's 'Whatever in the world could you be?' line.-->
									<attribute id="DialogNodeId" type="guid" value="c772a7ae-3a5d-4403-8c6c-55be48086d6d" />
									<attribute id="ReferenceId" type="guid" value="c772a7ae-3a5d-4403-8c6c-55be48086d6d" />
									<attribute id="PerformanceFade" type="double" value="0" />
									<attribute id="FadeIn" type="double" value="0" />
									<attribute id="FadeOut" type="double" value="0" />
									<children>
										<node id="Actor">
											<attribute id="UUID" type="guid" value="baa8d56b-3671-0f79-04fd-4345329cf311" />
										</node>
									</children>
								</node>

								<!--I've also changed the camera angle here, to look at the player instead of Astarion, so you can see the camera angle change.-->

								<node id="EffectComponent">
									<!--TLShot controls camera angles, and when the game uses and switches between them! An instance of TLShot is necessary for each phase in the timeline; the game won't be able to reference camera data without it!-->
									<attribute id="Type" type="LSString" value="TLShot" />
									<attribute id="ID" type="guid" value="e9f84b27-8b32-4cfb-bfc8-a45188bf5f6b" />
									<!--Unlike a lot of other effect components, TLShot isn't controlled by timing keys. Instead, the start and end time for the effect component controls when the camera is being used!
									
									Make sure that for any changes you make to the timing of of TLShot, you update the start and end times for the instances of TLShot around it, too. You want to make sure the StartTime of one TLShot component matches the EndTime of the previous TLShot component, and that there are no gaps or overlaps between them.-->
									<attribute id="StartTime" type="float" value="1224.5378" />
									<attribute id="EndTime" type="float" value="1231.116" />
									<attribute id="PhaseIndex" type="int64" value="94" />
									<attribute id="IsSnappedToEnd" type="bool" value="True" />
									<children>
                                        <!--This is the UUID of the camera that'll be used! It'll link to a scenecam entry in the TimelineActorData section. Swap this UUID with another scenecam to change the camera being used!
										
										A little note: BG3's cutscenes and dialog rely on continuitiy editing, so having a good understanding of it will help a lot when editing scenes! There's a ton of resources out there about the kind of cinematic language and editing style BG3 uses, and I really recommend looking them up if you're not familiar.
										
										The cool thing about working with dialog in this way is that you're essentially video editing on the XML level!!! And having a good understanding of it will go a long way.-->
										<node id="CameraContainer">
											<attribute id="Object" type="guid" value="57456c79-7ea8-4a5f-b5ad-927d1398e51e" />
										</node>
									</children>
								</node>


                                <!--End Dialog Tutorial Edits-->

